WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.033 --> 00:00:00.300
A federal

00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:01.801
judge overseeing the former president's

00:00:01.801 --> 00:00:02.969
indictment on election

00:00:02.969 --> 00:00:04.804
interference has denied his attempt

00:00:04.804 --> 00:00:05.905
to dismiss charges

00:00:05.905 --> 00:00:06.573
based on a claim

00:00:06.573 --> 00:00:08.074
of presidential immunity.

00:00:08.074 --> 00:00:09.709
At one point in her ruling,

00:00:09.709 --> 00:00:10.677
the judge, Tanya

00:00:10.677 --> 00:00:11.544
Chutkan, writes,

00:00:11.544 --> 00:00:13.380
quote, Defendants for your service

00:00:13.380 --> 00:00:14.647
as commander in chief

00:00:14.647 --> 00:00:15.682
do not bestow on him

00:00:15.682 --> 00:00:16.116
the divine

00:00:16.116 --> 00:00:16.916
right of kings

00:00:16.916 --> 00:00:18.451
to evade the criminal accountability

00:00:18.451 --> 00:00:20.587
that governs his fellow citizens.

00:00:20.587 --> 00:00:21.621
This decision comes

00:00:21.621 --> 00:00:23.390
the same day as another setback

00:00:23.390 --> 00:00:24.190
for the former president

00:00:24.190 --> 00:00:25.859
in federal court in Washington.

00:00:25.859 --> 00:00:27.193
A three judge appeals court

00:00:27.193 --> 00:00:27.961
panel decided

00:00:27.961 --> 00:00:29.162
he can be sued in

00:00:29.162 --> 00:00:31.197
civil court related to his actions

00:00:31.197 --> 00:00:32.132
during the January 6

00:00:32.132 --> 00:00:33.366
riot at the Capitol.

00:00:33.366 --> 00:00:34.901
The decision was unanimous

00:00:34.901 --> 00:00:35.769
to distinguish

00:00:35.769 --> 00:00:37.237
between campaign speech

00:00:37.237 --> 00:00:39.572
and official actions of a president.

00:00:39.572 --> 00:00:40.774
It's a victory for the Capitol

00:00:40.774 --> 00:00:42.442
Police and lawmakers behind

00:00:42.442 --> 00:00:43.410
three separate cases

00:00:43.410 --> 00:00:44.744
affected by the decision,

00:00:44.744 --> 00:00:45.378
plus others

00:00:45.378 --> 00:00:47.881
who may now seek civil damages as well.

00:00:47.881 --> 00:00:48.882
Perspective on all this

00:00:48.882 --> 00:00:49.983
from CNN political analysts

00:00:49.983 --> 00:00:51.017
Maggie Haberman, senior

00:00:51.017 --> 00:00:51.818
political correspondent

00:00:51.818 --> 00:00:52.652
for The New York Times,

00:00:52.652 --> 00:00:54.654
also the author of Confidence Man

00:00:54.654 --> 00:00:55.789
The Making of Donald Trump

00:00:55.789 --> 00:00:57.123
and the Breaking of America.

00:00:57.123 --> 00:01:00.427
Also, Caroline Polisi, she's White House.

00:01:00.493 --> 00:01:01.227
She's, excuse me,

00:01:01.227 --> 00:01:02.796
a white collar criminal defense attorney.

00:01:02.796 --> 00:01:03.930
She's also a lecturer

00:01:03.930 --> 00:01:05.498
at Columbia Law School.

00:01:05.498 --> 00:01:06.332
How big, Maggie,

00:01:06.332 --> 00:01:08.668
of a blow to the former

00:01:08.668 --> 00:01:10.203
president is this?

00:01:10.203 --> 00:01:11.538
Look, it was always a long shot

00:01:11.538 --> 00:01:13.173
that this is going to go through

00:01:13.173 --> 00:01:13.673
or the shutdown

00:01:13.673 --> 00:01:15.041
was going to roll on his side.

00:01:15.041 --> 00:01:17.043
She's made very clear in previous rulings

00:01:17.043 --> 00:01:18.778
how she views some of the claims

00:01:18.778 --> 00:01:19.946
that Trump has been making

00:01:19.946 --> 00:01:21.214
or his lawyers have been making.

00:01:21.214 --> 00:01:22.682
But what this does does do

00:01:22.682 --> 00:01:24.617
is it starts the clock on an appeal

00:01:24.617 --> 00:01:25.952
that they are going to have

00:01:25.952 --> 00:01:27.153
go through the courts,

00:01:27.153 --> 00:01:29.923
possibly go up to the Supreme Court.

00:01:30.023 --> 00:01:30.990
No one knows how the

00:01:30.990 --> 00:01:31.958
Supreme Court will rule

00:01:31.958 --> 00:01:33.093
if they will even take it up.

00:01:33.093 --> 00:01:33.960
They don't have to.

00:01:33.960 --> 00:01:35.462
They have generally not sided

00:01:35.462 --> 00:01:37.530
with Trump on any of his election

00:01:37.530 --> 00:01:38.431
related issues.

00:01:38.431 --> 00:01:40.467
They obviously have on other issues.

00:01:40.467 --> 00:01:42.001
If they send this back

00:01:42.001 --> 00:01:44.137
or if they rule against him.

00:01:44.137 --> 00:01:46.372
The clock then starts on the trial.

00:01:46.372 --> 00:01:48.608
But this buys time for his team.

00:01:48.608 --> 00:01:50.176
So this is not a surprising ruling,

00:01:50.176 --> 00:01:52.512
but it is a very, very lengthy ruling.

00:01:52.512 --> 00:01:55.215
And it refers to the Nixon pardon.

00:01:55.215 --> 00:01:57.150
It refers to a number of things

00:01:57.150 --> 00:01:58.051
that counter

00:01:58.051 --> 00:01:59.652
what Trump's team is arguing.

00:01:59.652 --> 00:02:00.720
Carolina, what stood out to you

00:02:00.720 --> 00:02:01.554
in these rulings?

00:02:01.554 --> 00:02:03.356
Yeah, I think, look, earlier today

00:02:03.356 --> 00:02:04.324
when the D.C.

00:02:04.324 --> 00:02:06.626
Circuit came out with the ruling

00:02:06.626 --> 00:02:08.461
with respect to the civil context,

00:02:08.461 --> 00:02:10.096
that was an easier bar to meet.

00:02:10.096 --> 00:02:12.432
The presidential immunity is really,

00:02:12.432 --> 00:02:12.699
you know,

00:02:12.699 --> 00:02:13.900
a thing that has been recognized

00:02:13.900 --> 00:02:14.767
by the Supreme Court

00:02:14.767 --> 00:02:16.803
since Nixon versus Fitzgerald.

00:02:16.803 --> 00:02:17.904
Trump was trying to push it

00:02:17.904 --> 00:02:19.939
further in the criminal context.

00:02:19.939 --> 00:02:22.275
Not surprising that check in waited,

00:02:22.275 --> 00:02:23.143
I think, for

00:02:23.143 --> 00:02:24.377
sort of her superiors

00:02:24.377 --> 00:02:25.211
to come out with

00:02:25.211 --> 00:02:27.347
with the ruling this afternoon.

00:02:27.347 --> 00:02:29.315
And then immediately I agree with Maggie.

00:02:29.315 --> 00:02:29.949
I think she's

00:02:29.949 --> 00:02:31.184
she wants to keep that March

00:02:31.184 --> 00:02:32.519
4th trial date.

00:02:32.519 --> 00:02:33.520
This is the one thing

00:02:33.520 --> 00:02:34.320
that could potentially

00:02:34.320 --> 00:02:36.523
throw a wrench in those plans

00:02:36.523 --> 00:02:38.291
if the case is somehow stayed

00:02:38.291 --> 00:02:39.492
pending an appeal.

00:02:39.492 --> 00:02:40.527
Certainly,

00:02:40.527 --> 00:02:42.128
I think he certainly will appeal

00:02:42.128 --> 00:02:44.464
this ruling as well as the D.C.

00:02:44.464 --> 00:02:45.298
Circuit ruling.

00:02:45.298 --> 00:02:47.033
And I think it is right for

00:02:47.033 --> 00:02:48.101
Supreme Court review.

00:02:48.101 --> 00:02:49.135
So do you think it's likely

00:02:49.135 --> 00:02:50.770
it would be stayed?

00:02:50.770 --> 00:02:51.437
You know,

00:02:51.437 --> 00:02:52.405
just as Maggie was saying,

00:02:52.405 --> 00:02:52.805
you never know

00:02:52.805 --> 00:02:53.840
what the Supreme Court is going to do.

00:02:53.840 --> 00:02:54.340
They can take it.

00:02:54.340 --> 00:02:55.341
They cannot they could stay.

00:02:55.341 --> 00:02:56.643
They could not.

00:02:56.643 --> 00:02:58.912
But I think that that is sort of

00:02:58.912 --> 00:03:00.180
the question mark here

00:03:00.180 --> 00:03:01.214
with respect to that.

00:03:01.214 --> 00:03:02.682
It's looking like more and more like

00:03:02.682 --> 00:03:04.050
that's going to be the only trial

00:03:04.050 --> 00:03:06.152
that will sort of get in under the gun

00:03:06.152 --> 00:03:08.087
before the election.

00:03:08.087 --> 00:03:09.022
Is it clear to you, Maggie,

00:03:09.022 --> 00:03:10.557
what other

00:03:10.557 --> 00:03:12.091
arguments the president might make to try

00:03:12.091 --> 00:03:13.226
to get this thrown out?

00:03:13.226 --> 00:03:14.260
I think this was a big one.

00:03:14.260 --> 00:03:16.396
I think if I think getting it thrown out

00:03:16.396 --> 00:03:17.397
is going to be very, very hard.

00:03:17.397 --> 00:03:18.231
This was really

00:03:18.231 --> 00:03:19.966
if this was the shot, it's possible

00:03:19.966 --> 00:03:21.534
someone was suggesting to me today

00:03:21.534 --> 00:03:22.235
that the Supreme Court

00:03:22.235 --> 00:03:24.404
could take up the gag order issue

00:03:24.404 --> 00:03:25.371
that seems a little less

00:03:25.371 --> 00:03:26.439
likely than this one,

00:03:26.439 --> 00:03:26.973
just because this is

00:03:26.973 --> 00:03:28.208
a presidential power issue

00:03:28.208 --> 00:03:29.576
and it's a little broader

00:03:29.576 --> 00:03:30.977
that the other one is specific

00:03:30.977 --> 00:03:31.978
to Trump as a defendant.

00:03:31.978 --> 00:03:32.979
I think this is it.

00:03:32.979 --> 00:03:33.980
In terms of their shot

00:03:33.980 --> 00:03:35.615
of getting it thrown out entirely.

00:03:35.615 --> 00:03:37.483
Next up becomes just,

00:03:37.483 --> 00:03:38.751
you know, trying for an acquittal

00:03:38.751 --> 00:03:41.087
or trying for a hung jury

00:03:41.087 --> 00:03:43.323
or trying that those are their best hope.

00:03:43.323 --> 00:03:46.359
This is this is a case that being tried

00:03:46.359 --> 00:03:49.996
in D.C., Trump's allies and advisers

00:03:50.096 --> 00:03:52.232
think is unlikely to go his way

00:03:52.232 --> 00:03:53.433
just based on the events

00:03:53.433 --> 00:03:55.735
and based on what the jury pool will be.

00:03:55.735 --> 00:03:56.803
But that's down the road.

00:03:56.803 --> 00:03:57.704
There was also, Maggie,

00:03:57.704 --> 00:04:00.006
the pretrial hearing in the Georgia

00:04:00.006 --> 00:04:01.841
election case, which stood out either.

00:04:01.841 --> 00:04:03.176
Well, it was interesting

00:04:03.176 --> 00:04:04.010
listening to this argument

00:04:04.010 --> 00:04:04.944
that the trial ought to start.

00:04:04.944 --> 00:04:05.378
I think it was

00:04:05.378 --> 00:04:06.512
there was some suggestion

00:04:06.512 --> 00:04:08.114
it should start in 2029

00:04:08.114 --> 00:04:08.881
or something like that.

00:04:08.881 --> 00:04:09.782
I mean, well, well,

00:04:09.782 --> 00:04:11.351
well down the road,

00:04:11.351 --> 00:04:12.085
what you've heard over

00:04:12.085 --> 00:04:13.486
and over again from the Trump lawyers

00:04:13.486 --> 00:04:15.421
is there's such a volume of discovery.

00:04:15.421 --> 00:04:16.823
This is such an exotic case.

00:04:16.823 --> 00:04:18.658
And they've said this in various ones.

00:04:18.658 --> 00:04:20.827
We need time to go through everything

00:04:20.827 --> 00:04:21.861
for discovery.

00:04:21.861 --> 00:04:23.496
We need time to look at the evidence

00:04:23.496 --> 00:04:24.163
in the march.

00:04:24.163 --> 00:04:25.298
A lot of the documents case,

00:04:25.298 --> 00:04:26.733
there are clearance issues there.

00:04:26.733 --> 00:04:27.634
There actually are on the January

00:04:27.634 --> 00:04:28.434
six case, too,

00:04:28.434 --> 00:04:30.903
although it's a little less so

00:04:30.970 --> 00:04:32.505
that it doesn't surprise me

00:04:32.505 --> 00:04:34.040
that they're talking about a delay.

00:04:34.040 --> 00:04:35.141
A delay of that much

00:04:35.141 --> 00:04:36.109
was surprising to me.

00:04:36.109 --> 00:04:38.311
And I would be mostly surprised

00:04:38.311 --> 00:04:39.879
if it works in Georgia.

00:04:39.879 --> 00:04:41.214
The president's attorneys are arguing

00:04:41.214 --> 00:04:42.482
that this violates Trump's

00:04:42.482 --> 00:04:43.283
free speech rights.

00:04:43.283 --> 00:04:43.583
Yeah,

00:04:43.583 --> 00:04:44.384
And they by the way,

00:04:44.384 --> 00:04:45.852
they made that argument today

00:04:45.852 --> 00:04:47.053
in the check in motion as well,

00:04:47.053 --> 00:04:48.021
which which she denied.

00:04:48.021 --> 00:04:49.956
But the 2029 date, you know,

00:04:49.956 --> 00:04:52.759
that was under the scenario in which

00:04:52.759 --> 00:04:53.893
the judge asked

00:04:53.893 --> 00:04:55.461
Trump lawyers, well, what would happen

00:04:55.461 --> 00:04:56.996
if he were to be elected president?

00:04:56.996 --> 00:04:57.597
Essentially,

00:04:57.597 --> 00:04:59.799
it would stop the clock on

00:04:59.799 --> 00:05:01.634
that that time to prosecute.

00:05:01.634 --> 00:05:03.703
But I have a different perspective

00:05:03.703 --> 00:05:04.737
because I'm the defense attorney.

00:05:04.737 --> 00:05:07.573
I think the August 2024 date

00:05:07.573 --> 00:05:08.675
is a bit aggressive.

00:05:08.675 --> 00:05:09.409
There are

00:05:09.409 --> 00:05:10.243
there's a backlog

00:05:10.243 --> 00:05:12.612
in the criminal in Fulton County, Georgia

00:05:12.612 --> 00:05:14.414
Criminal Court.

00:05:14.414 --> 00:05:15.682
And, you know, it cuts both ways.

00:05:15.682 --> 00:05:17.050
Any criminal defendant

00:05:17.050 --> 00:05:19.152
shouldn't be above the law.

00:05:19.152 --> 00:05:20.653
I think finally,

00:05:20.653 --> 00:05:21.354
Wallace is trying

00:05:21.354 --> 00:05:22.522
to push this case through.

00:05:22.522 --> 00:05:23.356
She wants to get it in

00:05:23.356 --> 00:05:24.390
before the election.

00:05:24.390 --> 00:05:26.492
I think it's pretty apparent. Yeah.

00:05:26.492 --> 00:05:27.593
Which of these cases

00:05:27.593 --> 00:05:28.728
do you think the former president

00:05:28.728 --> 00:05:30.463
is most concerned about?

00:05:30.463 --> 00:05:31.097
I think he's concerned

00:05:31.097 --> 00:05:32.298
about all of them, honestly.

00:05:32.298 --> 00:05:33.433
I think that he's more concerned

00:05:33.433 --> 00:05:34.434
about the federal ones.

00:05:34.434 --> 00:05:35.702
The the documents case

00:05:35.702 --> 00:05:36.903
in particular concerns him,

00:05:36.903 --> 00:05:38.304
except for the judge in that case,

00:05:38.304 --> 00:05:39.706
which is one of his own appointees.

00:05:39.706 --> 00:05:41.240
And the fact that it's a more favorable

00:05:41.240 --> 00:05:42.909
jury pool just based on the counties

00:05:42.909 --> 00:05:44.243
around the courthouse.

00:05:44.243 --> 00:05:46.746
The January six case angers him

00:05:46.746 --> 00:05:48.381
for a variety of reasons.

00:05:48.381 --> 00:05:50.216
And you can see it when he talks

00:05:50.216 --> 00:05:52.585
about the election, relates to

00:05:52.585 --> 00:05:55.254
an event that he considered humiliating,

00:05:55.254 --> 00:05:56.756
which is having to leave the White House.

00:05:56.756 --> 00:05:58.024
And so I think all of these things

00:05:58.024 --> 00:05:59.225
tie together.

00:05:59.225 --> 00:06:01.427
He's angry about the Manhattan indictment

00:06:01.427 --> 00:06:02.729
for different reasons,

00:06:02.729 --> 00:06:03.663
and there's no case

00:06:03.663 --> 00:06:04.964
that makes him feel good here.

00:06:04.964 --> 00:06:06.332
They're all they're all bad,

00:06:06.332 --> 00:06:07.266
but they are most

00:06:07.266 --> 00:06:09.168
they're most concerned right now

00:06:09.168 --> 00:06:10.403
about the January 6th one

00:06:10.403 --> 00:06:11.671
because they think that's the one

00:06:11.671 --> 00:06:12.105
that's likely.

00:06:12.105 --> 00:06:13.039
It's nice to me

00:06:13.039 --> 00:06:14.040
the humiliation is having

00:06:14.040 --> 00:06:14.674
to leave the White House

00:06:14.674 --> 00:06:15.942
as opposed to the humiliation

00:06:15.942 --> 00:06:17.443
of having his supporters

00:06:17.443 --> 00:06:18.478
break into the Congress.

00:06:18.478 --> 00:06:20.513
And we've heard him defend that.

00:06:20.513 --> 00:06:21.614
We've heard him defend that.

00:06:21.614 --> 00:06:22.982
So that is not something

00:06:22.982 --> 00:06:24.650
that I have heard him sound

00:06:24.650 --> 00:06:26.419
any any concession of shame

00:06:26.419 --> 00:06:28.421
about publicly to the point

00:06:28.421 --> 00:06:29.722
about fairness versus

00:06:29.722 --> 00:06:30.790
a speedy trial, though,

00:06:30.790 --> 00:06:32.425
I think that you are going to hear that

00:06:32.425 --> 00:06:33.526
over and over again.

00:06:33.526 --> 00:06:34.961
And it is the one place

00:06:34.961 --> 00:06:36.996
or a place where the Trump team

00:06:36.996 --> 00:06:38.498
has a legitimate point

00:06:38.498 --> 00:06:39.866
about the fact that Trump does

00:06:39.866 --> 00:06:40.733
have the same rights

00:06:40.733 --> 00:06:41.968
as any other defendant.

00:06:41.968 --> 00:06:43.269
Caroline, thanks so much.

00:06:43.269 --> 00:06:43.836
Maggie Haberman.

